WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.949
Security telemetry should record events
that help reconstruct meaningful behavior.

2
00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:07.893
Examples include authentication success
and failure,

3
00:00:07.973 --> 00:00:10.899
session creation or revocation,
authorization denial,

4
00:00:10.979 --> 00:00:14.209
administrative operations, role changes,
account recovery,

5
00:00:14.289 --> 00:00:16.970
API-key lifecycle events, important data
exports,

6
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.707
destructive operations, security-relevant
configuration changes,

7
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.505
rate-limit enforcement, and validation
failures that indicate abuse.

8
00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:27.632
Successful privileged actions matter as
much as failures;

9
00:00:27.712 --> 00:00:31.917
an audit trail that records only denied
operations omits changes an authorized or

10
00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:33.704
compromised account completed.

11
00:00:33.784 --> 00:00:38.050
Useful events identify the relevant
dimensions without collecting entire domain

12
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:38.617
objects.

13
00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:42.293
Depending on the purpose, fields may
include an authenticated actor,

14
00:00:42.373 --> 00:00:45.664
tenant, resource, operation, server-side
timestamp, result,

15
00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:49.462
request identifier, trace identifier, and
an error or reason category.

16
00:00:49.542 --> 00:00:53.381
Distinguish authentication failure,
legitimate authorization denial,

17
00:00:53.461 --> 00:00:57.605
validation rejection, dependency failure,
and internal authorization error.

18
00:00:57.685 --> 00:01:01.891
Collapsing them into a generic
access-failed message weakens alerting and makes

19
00:01:01.971 --> 00:01:03.372
outages look like attacks.

20
00:01:03.452 --> 00:01:07.779
Keep routine high-volume behavior at an
appropriate level so important signals are

21
00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:08.469
not buried.

22
00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:12.937
Correlation identifiers connect records;
they do not prove identity or permission.

23
00:01:13.017 --> 00:01:16.613
An inbound request ID from a standard
header is still untrusted input.

24
00:01:16.693 --> 00:01:18.704
Validate its syntax, bound its length,

25
00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:23.172
and decide whether to normalize it or
replace it with a server-generated identifier.

26
00:01:23.252 --> 00:01:27.152
When retaining a client value is useful
for cross-system troubleshooting,

27
00:01:27.232 --> 00:01:31.498
consider keeping it in a separate bounded
field while using a server-controlled ID

28
00:01:31.578 --> 00:01:33.224
as the primary correlation key.

29
00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:37.509
Session correlation should use an approved
surrogate rather than the raw session

30
00:01:37.589 --> 00:01:38.260
credential.

31
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.716
Context propagation should also be
explicit.

32
00:01:40.796 --> 00:01:44.270
A request-scoped child logger can bind
approved identifiers once,

33
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.495
reducing accidental inconsistency, while
individual events add the resource,

34
00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:49.672
action, and outcome.

35
00:01:49.752 --> 00:01:53.957
The bound context must come from trusted
authentication and tenancy processing,

36
00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:56.353
not directly from arbitrary request
fields.

37
00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:00.516
This pattern produces coherent evidence
across handlers and services without

38
00:02:00.596 --> 00:02:04.923
confusing correlation with authorization
or expanding every log call into a dump of

39
00:02:05.003 --> 00:02:06.100
the request context.
